aged through the use of seed  treatments which are not approved  materials for organic growers and so  that's an instance where disease  resistant where genetic resistance would  be more important a higher priority for  organic growers so for organic  production to be particularly successful  growers need varieties that excel under  organic conditions which means that we  need to be trialing varieties under  organics management if they're meant for  organic growers or develop varieties  that are intended specifically for  organics so how do we develop those  varieties well one of the ways that I've  been thinking about it is a matter of  adaptability and stability under  different environments so this graph  from Finley and Wilkinson shows shows a  trial of barley varieties under  different environments and the x-axis  shows the mean yield of all of the  varieties in seven different  environments and then every variety  their yield in those environments is  plotted so so the average yield of all  the varieties is the dashed line here  with a slope of one and varieties that  that these authors label as well-adapted  or generally adapted are those that do  well under all environments do better  than average under all environment so  variety a in this instance variety B is  an example of a poorly adapted variety  it does worse than the average across  environments variety C  is a variety with low stability so it  does particularly poorly in low yielding  environments but well when it's when  it's in a good a good field generally  and the particularly interesting for for  my purposes is variety D here which is a  variety with high stability so it does  well it does pretty well under low and  are low yielding environments but it  does it does the same generally across  those environments so the authors of  this paper conclude that selecting for  for high yielding varieties under high  yielding conditions if you don't know  how those varieties do under low  yielding conditions can result in  varieties that don't particularly do  well under those yields low yielding  environments which we can think of as  being representative perhaps of organics  management conditions so four varieties  that do well under good conditions those  might not be appropriate for organic  growers and dr. Kevin Murphy  demonstrated this again in a study that  was done in 2007 with wheat varieties  where they under in five different  environments looked at the yield of  these varieties under conventional and  organic management and then ranked them  according to yield and showed showed  that the yield rank changed quite  drastically between conventional and  organic conditions suggesting that if  this was in a breeding program selecting  for the lines that did well under  conventional management would not have  resulted in in lines that were that good  for organic so those that did the best  under organic management were the ones  that would have been chosen under  conventional systems so there there are  some traits that are generally important  to organic growers those that are  dictated by the standards and then there  are traits that are region or market  specific which also need to be  considered when we supply  we're looking to support a particular  community of growers so in this instance  some of the work that I've done has been  particularly to the Northeast and  organic growers here I spoke last year  about a report that we were writing that  that summed up results of a survey that  we did asking growers vegetable growers  here in the Northeast who were using  organic practices what was most  important to them and what the traits  were that they would like to suggest be  worked on to support their production  and so part of the reports that we put  out last August was a prioritization of  many specific crop specific trait needs  for organic growers and the top five  sets are listed here so those that are  under bullet point three were all deemed  to