 been looking at powdery mildew  resistance and Cornell Bush delicata in  the far right bottom corner is the  source of powdery mildew resistance that  I used I a couple of summers ago crossed  it with a couple of commercial  commercial lines with higher quality  these three boxes here which have as you  can see from last year quite a lot  higher severity of powdery mildew and  the breeding lines that I've been  working on I was able to fix the powdery  mildew resistance using a capsule marker  that was developed in our lab and all of  those show very low powdery mildew  severity in the field which is  encouraging I also have been looking at  screening the material for bricks as a  an assay for quality and then the second  breeding project that I've been working  on is an early blocky bell pepper and  growers here in the Northeast or gay  vegetable growers often grow this  variety which is called ace and folks  have said that they they like the  variety it produces quite a lot of food  it produces the chair food earlier than  other varieties that they've grown but  it could use some changes in the fruit  shape I've heard several people  independently tell me that they think it  looks really ugly which maybe shouldn't  matter but apparently it does so so I've  been working on that and I crossed ace  to a blockier bell pepper called  Aristotle these were both hybrids and  last year I grew f2 families which  sounds weird to say and looked for early  earliness in these these lines so the  parents are ace and Aristotle and this  is showing the percent of the plot that  was flowering on July 6th so ace had  already passed the 50% point and  Aristotle was still not really flowering  identified four lines that were almost  as early as ace if not earlier and then  looked for blocky fruit and have  identified two promising lines that will  be trailing along with the delicata here  in free ville and on some organic farms  here in the Northeast so in New York as  well as at our